.TH TARTEST 1
.SH NAME
tartest \- regression test for tar(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tartest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tartest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/tar.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(argv) ,
and checks it against expected output for nine cases, each built from
either a hand-constructed ustar/PAX/GNU-longname byte sequence under
.B /tartest
or a real directory tree round-tripped through
.BR tar 's
own create and extract verbs together, cleaned up whether or not a
case fails. See
.IR tar (1)
and
.B tar.b 's
own header comment for the translation decisions each case targets.
.PP
Cases worth calling out specifically:
.TP
.B T5
Builds a PAX extended header naming
.B path=paxlongname.txt
ahead of a regular ustar header whose own
.B name
field is the short placeholder
.BR shortname.txt ,
then checks that extraction used the PAX name, not the ustar one, and
that no file named
.B shortname.txt
was created at all. Confirmed sensitive with a negative control:
temporarily disabling the PAX
.B path=
override in
.B parsepax()
made this case fail exactly as predicted (the placeholder name was
used instead), restored afterward.
.TP
.B T7 / T8
The same corrupted-checksum header, once without resync (expects a
hard failure) and once with
.B -s
given (expects
.IR readhdrblk ()
to skip block-by-block past the bad header and its data block and
recover the next good one). Diagnostic noise from the resync scan
itself (it briefly treats the skipped data block as a candidate
header and prints a bogus "empty checksum" warning while doing so) is
expected and matches what the original's own resync loop would print
in the same situation \(em not a bug.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/tartest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tar (1)
.SH BUGS
The stronger oracle \(em a real host
.B tar
(bsdtar/libarchive) reading archives this
.B tar
wrote, and vice versa, in both plain-ustar and PAX-default formats,
including a 130-character filename in both directions \(em was run by
hand outside emu during development, not as part of this automated
suite: Limbo has no fork/exec, so a test running inside emu cannot
invoke a host binary at all. The exact commands and results are
recorded in this file's own header comment as a permanent record
instead of a re-run one.
.PP
Relatedly, an attempt to verify the
.IR tar (1)-documented
uid/gid field-swap bug via a real extract-with-\c
.B -T
-then-restat round trip was tried and abandoned: this host filesystem
backend's
.B fwstat
rejects setting an arbitrary string gid outright (confirmed by a
throwaway probe, not assumed), making that check environment-dependent
rather than a reliable regression test. The bug itself is still
real and still documented \(em see
.IR tar (1)'s
own BUGS \(em just not exercised here.
.PP
A case that raises through
.BR fail ()
partway through (as happens if a fixture is built wrong, or during the
negative control above) skips that case's own end-of-function cleanup,
same as every other run()-seam test in this tree \(em a stray fixture
under
.B /tartest
from an aborted run can leak into the next one. Encountered once
during this file's own development (cleaned up by hand, not a defect
in a passing run) and noted here rather than papered over.
.PP
Does not exercise
.B tar r
(append to an existing archive),
.BR -k / -R / -i ,
the base-256 huge-file-size encoding path, or the
.B LF_PAXGLOBL
(global, as opposed to per-file) PAX header kind \(em
.B T5
only builds a per-file PAX header. All translated in
.BR tar.b ,
none independently verified to the same depth as the cases above.
.PP
Every fixture file in T1\-T9 is small enough to round-trip in a single
512-byte block. A later advisor review caught that
.IR addtoar ()
originally read file content with a single
.B sys->read()
call per block rather than
.BR sys->readn () ,
which would have silently corrupted any archived file large enough to
receive a short read partway through \(em fixed, but a genuine negative
control (reverting the fix and archiving a 2000-byte/4-block file)
did NOT reproduce a failure here: this host's regular-file reads never
come back short in practice, so the fix is real by
.BR sys->readn ()'s
own documented contract and by matching the original's
.BR ereadn (),
not by an observed corruption in this environment. See
.IR tar (1)'s
own note.
